WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.780
- Live TV means you can't plan
for what's about to happen.

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:06.780
And sometimes there's some
serious medical surprises.

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:07.770
Let's watch it together.

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:08.910
Be whoop.

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:10.701
- [Announcer] It looks
like Ruben is in the lead

00:00:10.701 --> 00:00:11.534
and here it goes.

00:00:11.534 --> 00:00:12.540
- Oh, wipe out.

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:13.373
Whoa!

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:15.780
That could be a knee injury,

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:18.600
an ankle injury and a head
injury and a broken rib

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:19.500
all in one.

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:22.530
- Riverside Park, Rob Black, Global News.

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:23.670
- Great sign off, though.

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:25.140
The fall was so bad it reminds me

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:27.142
of Johnny Knoxville's thing with the bull.

00:00:27.142 --> 00:00:31.045
- It's time for our How you
Doing Halloween Costume Contest.

00:00:31.045 --> 00:00:32.130
We do it every year.

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:32.963
It's always a lot of fun.

00:00:32.963 --> 00:00:34.530
Let's get started.

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:35.680
Our first (indistinct).

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:41.474
- Oh, oh!

00:00:41.474 --> 00:00:44.700
(woman gasping)

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:46.045
- Oh!

00:00:46.045 --> 00:00:46.878
- [Announcer] The show cut to black

00:00:46.878 --> 00:00:48.930
and went to an extra
long commercial break.

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:52.860
But when it did come back
on, Wendy was back up.

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:54.930
- That was not a stunt.

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:57.600
I'm overheated in my
costume and I did pass out,

00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:58.860
but, you know what?

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:00.930
I'm a champ and I'm back.

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:02.130
- Get evaluated.

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:03.870
Overheating is a real issue.

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:05.430
Dehydrating is a real issue.

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:07.650
Heat stroke is a real
issue and it shouldn't be,

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:09.420
oh, I just had a heat stroke, I fainted,

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:10.800
I'm good to go now.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:12.000
That's not how it works.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:12.833
Okay.

00:01:12.833 --> 00:01:14.340
A little dancing, a little dancing!

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:17.444
My dance moves are just as good as this.

00:01:17.444 --> 00:01:18.277
(dance music)

00:01:18.277 --> 00:01:19.860
- Yeah.

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:21.610
- What can go wrong medically here?

00:01:24.733 --> 00:01:25.792
- You okay?

00:01:25.792 --> 00:01:26.625
- Think someone got hurt.

00:01:26.625 --> 00:01:28.613
- [Dancer] My knee, my knee snapped.

00:01:28.613 --> 00:01:31.140
- Usually when patients
describe a knee snapping

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:32.400
they mean the knee gave out.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.940
Most commonly this can happen
with a meniscus or an ACL.

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:37.740
Sometimes a PCL injury
if there's some kind

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:39.688
of weird collision that happened.

00:01:39.688 --> 00:01:41.564
- I didn't fall.

00:01:41.564 --> 00:01:43.413
- I was catching her weight.

00:01:46.043 --> 00:01:47.940
- Oh, I see what happened.

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:49.009
Okay.

00:01:49.009 --> 00:01:50.700
So there's potential for
a meniscus injury there

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:52.740
as well as an MCL injury.

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:54.960
At that moment, if you have
a tear of that ligament

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:57.540
you're just gonna get so
much swelling and bruising.

00:01:57.540 --> 00:01:58.373
He's gonna get an x-ray

00:01:58.373 --> 00:01:59.970
to make sure that no bones are broken.

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:01.170
He's gonna get some crutches.

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:02.820
He's gonna get follow up, get an MRI.

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:05.730
Find out that the medial
meniscus and the MCL are torn.

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:08.190
Potentially either do
rehab and/or surgery.

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:10.656
And will be back on his feet
dancing his little heart away.

00:02:10.656 --> 00:02:11.670
(tense music)

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:13.010
- The problem is...

00:02:14.312 --> 00:02:15.145
(woman crashing)

00:02:15.145 --> 00:02:15.978
- Oh!

00:02:15.978 --> 00:02:19.623
I did not expect a face
first plummet like that.

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:22.043
Call for help.

00:02:22.043 --> 00:02:23.580
First thing that we do, call for help

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:24.750
before even investigating.

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:26.070
And the microphone's still in her hand.

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:27.840
The first thing you want
to know is making sure

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:28.980
that the person is conscious.

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:29.813
Are they breathing?

00:02:29.813 --> 00:02:30.646
Do they have a pulse?

00:02:30.646 --> 00:02:32.400
Remember if there's no
pulse, you call 9-1-1

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:33.750
and you start those chest compressions,

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:35.160
chest compressions chest compressions.

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:36.570
But it looks like she's kind of with it.

00:02:36.570 --> 00:02:39.900
So my prediction here and
suspicion, is that she had

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:42.540
something known as a
vasovagal syncopal episode,

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:45.090
where, because of nerves,
because of extreme tension

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:48.630
her blood vessels dilated,
her heart rate dropped,

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:51.060
and as a result she wasn't
bringing enough circulation

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:51.893
to her brain.

00:02:51.893 --> 00:02:52.860
And she collapsed.

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:53.940
This is very dangerous,

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:56.640
not because of the actual
situation that's happening

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:57.990
with the low blood pressure.

00:02:57.990 --> 00:03:00.210
But the fall, the fall
is the dangerous part.

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:01.620
She fell face first.

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:03.280
- [EMT] So Karen, can you hear me?

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:05.550
- As an EMT, you're really in a bad spot

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:07.500
because you have no
idea what just happened.

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:08.730
The person isn't talking.

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:10.050
You're trying to get information.

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:10.883
You don't know,

00:03:10.883 --> 00:03:12.630
does this person have
underlying medical conditions?

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:13.860
So for their role,

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:15.810
the most important thing
is stabilizing the patient

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:16.860
getting them to an area

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:18.496
where they can receive adequate care.

00:03:18.496 --> 00:03:20.250
(siren blaring)

00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:22.170
She was treated for
dehydration and released

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:23.003
from the hospital.

00:03:23.003 --> 00:03:24.060
She will return for the showstopper round.

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:27.780
Okay, so dehydration can
precipitate a vasovagal episode

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:30.600
but it could also cause a
fainting episode all on its own.

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:32.460
Because what happens is
if you're dehydrated,

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:34.410
meaning you haven't
consumed enough beverages,

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:35.760
you don't have water content.

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:38.040
You actually lose a little
bit of blood volume.

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:39.210
And when that happens

00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:40.980
you can't generate enough pressure

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:43.890
to bring the oxygenated blood
to where it needs to go,

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:45.240
like the brain and the vital organs,

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:46.410
especially when you're standing.

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:48.330
And as a result, with
this blood pressure drop,

00:03:48.330 --> 00:03:50.730
you actually just faint
as a lifesaving mechanism.

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:52.290
Your brain wants you to go flat

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:53.130
because when you're flat,

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:55.200
the lower blood pressure
can still circulate

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:56.880
the oxygenated blood throughout your body.

00:03:56.880 --> 00:03:58.260
Correcting dehydration

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:01.500
in a severely dehydrated
individual is also very important

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:02.640
and needs to be done carefully.

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:04.290
'Cause if you rehydrate too quickly

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:06.000
with the incorrect type of fluids,

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:07.800
you can actually cause
swelling in the brain

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:09.150
and cause a patient to die that way.

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:10.950
There's a lot of things
that need to be taken

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:12.114
into account here.

00:04:12.114 --> 00:04:13.620
(piano playing)

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:14.920
- Ooh, she looks stressed.

00:04:17.083 --> 00:04:17.916
- [Panelist] She okay?

00:04:17.916 --> 00:04:18.749
- [2nd Panelist] You okay?

00:04:18.749 --> 00:04:21.230
(people talking excitedly)

00:04:21.230 --> 00:04:23.160
- Is she gonna throw up?

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:23.993
- Okay?

00:04:23.993 --> 00:04:25.710
- Again, very difficult to
figure out what's going on.

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:26.790
I like this position.

00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:28.740
It makes it easier for
the blood to return back

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:30.360
to the heart and go up to the brain.

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:34.023
- Makayla has condition
that's pretty rare.

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:38.340
- In seventh grade, I was
diagnosed with Vasovagal Syncope.

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:39.810
- That is not a rare condition.

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:40.890
Now maybe in individuals

00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:43.770
who it happens to quite
often is a rare condition.

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:46.140
There's two mechanisms at play
during this type of syncope.

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:49.620
One is increased
parasympathetic nerve output,

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:51.120
meaning that we're increasing

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:53.970
that rest-digest mechanism
nervous system in your body

00:04:53.970 --> 00:04:55.590
that opens up blood vessels,

00:04:55.590 --> 00:04:57.720
which is good when you're
lying down and relaxed.

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:00.030
Not good when you're trying
to perform physically

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:02.130
on stage because it
drops your blood pressure

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:04.500
therefore not bringing
oxygenated blood to the brain.

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:06.270
And then at the same time it shuts down

00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:08.040
sympathetic nerve firing.

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:10.260
So think about this, the blood
vessels in your legs expand.

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:11.460
So blood pressure drops.

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:14.250
Now, if your heart was allowed
to sympathetically respond

00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:16.140
it would increase its cardiac output

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:17.430
by beating faster.

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:19.110
As a result, you would be okay.

00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:20.910
You wouldn't even feel
that something's going on.

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:23.010
But because you're shutting
down the sympathetic

00:05:23.010 --> 00:05:25.530
and overdriving the parasympathetic

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:28.020
you're creating a really bad dis-imbalance

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:29.610
that causes someone to faint

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:31.920
and potentially really hurt themselves.

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:36.150
- Well, a very, very heavy,
heavy rotation tonight.

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:38.970
We had a very dare darison bite.

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:41.520
Let's go hit terace tazen
is in pavet that hamen pet.

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:42.570
- Do we know what happened here?

00:05:42.570 --> 00:05:43.800
Look like potential stroke.

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:45.270
- [Man Offscreen] She
claims it was a migraine.

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:47.760
- There are types of
migraines that could present

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:50.460
with neurologic symptoms
known as complex migraines.

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:52.020
I would want a full workup

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:53.940
to see if there was in fact a stroke.

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:55.950
Now in young, otherwise healthy people

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:57.510
sometimes the cause of
a stroke could happen

00:05:57.510 --> 00:05:58.770
as a result of a PFO

00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:00.750
that generally people don't
know about that they have.

00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:01.583
But as a result,

00:06:01.583 --> 00:06:03.210
if you ever have a blood clot in your leg

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:04.770
and it starts traveling to your heart

00:06:04.770 --> 00:06:07.050
it can transfer, instead
of going into your lungs,

00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:09.540
into the left side of your
heart, through that hole.

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:10.860
And then as a result gets shot up

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:12.420
to the brain leading to a stroke.

00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:14.190
- This is me 10 years ago.

00:06:14.190 --> 00:06:15.120
- Dan Harris

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:18.150
I've read this book where he
talks about this very moment.

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:19.890
- Researchers report people who take

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:22.050
cholesterol-lowering drugs called statins

00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:23.280
for at least five years

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:25.950
may also lower their risk for cancer,

00:06:25.950 --> 00:06:28.110
but it's too early to prescribe statins

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:29.610
slowly for cancer production.

00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:31.800
- [Dan Offscreen] At this
point, I realize I'm helpless.

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:34.023
So I bail right in the middle.

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:36.353
- Back to you.

00:06:36.353 --> 00:06:38.160
- [Announcer] We're gonna go
back now to Robin and Charlie.

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:40.800
- [Dan Offscreen] I knew
with rock solid certainty

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:43.860
that I had just had a panic
attack on national television.

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:46.020
- Panic attacks are a real phenomenon.

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:47.970
And people think that
they're all in their heads.

00:06:47.970 --> 00:06:50.340
Panic attacks may start in the mind,

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:52.500
but they continue out to
the rest of your body,

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:55.620
which means that you feel
like your heart is racing

00:06:55.620 --> 00:06:56.520
a million miles an hour.

00:06:56.520 --> 00:06:57.960
You can't take a deep breath.

00:06:57.960 --> 00:07:00.060
There's an impending feeling of doom.

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:02.490
You feel weak, you feel
numbness, tingling.

00:07:02.490 --> 00:07:04.440
That's why so many people will describe it

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:06.210
as a near heart attack episode.

00:07:06.210 --> 00:07:08.820
And sometimes we do wanna
rule out a heart attack.

00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:10.140
- To spend years in war zones

00:07:10.140 --> 00:07:12.780
where I covered the heroics
of our men and women overseas

00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:16.410
and got a real taste of both
the horror and the adrenaline

00:07:16.410 --> 00:07:19.800
of combat after years of
always barreling forward.

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:21.690
When I finally slowed down,

00:07:21.690 --> 00:07:25.230
it was as if my mind
revolted and I got depressed.

00:07:25.230 --> 00:07:26.760
And so in my free time,

00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:29.910
I briefly, but stupidly
began self-medicating

00:07:29.910 --> 00:07:32.550
and it was those recreational drugs,

00:07:32.550 --> 00:07:34.050
my doctor would later tell me,

00:07:34.050 --> 00:07:37.320
that almost certainly produced
the on-air panic attack.

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:39.960
- I wouldn't say it's
necessarily those drugs

00:07:39.960 --> 00:07:40.793
that caused it.

00:07:40.793 --> 00:07:42.210
It could have indirectly caused it

00:07:42.210 --> 00:07:44.490
by increasing the feelings of anxiety

00:07:44.490 --> 00:07:46.560
because a lot of people
will take feel good drugs

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:47.580
when they're depressed.

00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:49.650
And as a result, those
feel good drugs will mess

00:07:49.650 --> 00:07:50.940
with your neurotransmitters

00:07:50.940 --> 00:07:53.670
leading to higher highs
but also lower lows.

00:07:53.670 --> 00:07:55.230
And as a result, withdrawal symptoms

00:07:55.230 --> 00:07:57.510
that can make it feel like
you're having a panic attack

00:07:57.510 --> 00:07:59.250
or make it feel much worse.

00:07:59.250 --> 00:08:01.890
So much so that it could disrupt
you in your place of work.

00:08:01.890 --> 00:08:05.040
I do recommend you read
Dan Harris's "10% Happier".

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:07.320
Check it out on Audible, my partner.

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:09.990
- Stuff went wrong
during my own live event.

00:08:09.990 --> 00:08:11.640
My tour, my Alive Tour,

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:13.860
click here to check out
everything that went wrong.

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:17.643
As always, stay happy and
sorry for the haircut.

